  "full_response": "Software testing is a critical phase in the software development lifecycle (SDLC) that ensures the quality, functionality, and reliability of a product. Here are the main types of software testing, along with their purposes and when to use them:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Functional Testing**\n**Purpose:** Validates that the software functions as intended according to requirements.\n**When to use:** Throughout development, especially after implementing new features or fixing bugs.\n\n#### **Subtypes:**\n- **Unit Testing**\n  - **Purpose:** Tests individual components (e.g., functions, methods) in isolation.\n  - **When to use:** During development (often by developers using frameworks like JUnit, pytest).\n  - **Example:** Testing a single API endpoint or a login function.\n\n- **Integration Testing**\n  - **Purpose:** Tests interactions between integrated modules/components.\n  - **When to use:** After unit testing, before system testing (e.g., testing how a payment module interacts with a database).\n  - **Example:** Verifying that a frontend form correctly sends data to a backend service.\n\n- **System Testing**\n  - **Purpose:** Tests the complete system against specified requirements.\n  - **When to use:** After integration testing, before user acceptance testing (UAT).\n  - **Example:** Testing an e-commerce website end-to-end (from login to checkout).\n\n- **Acceptance Testing**\n  - **Purpose:** Validates the system against business requirements (often with stakeholders).\n  - **When to use:** Before release, often performed by clients or end-users.\n  - **Subtypes:**\n    - **User Acceptance Testing (UAT):** End-users test the system in a real-world scenario.\n    - **Alpha/Beta Testing:** Alpha (internal testing), Beta (external users test pre-release versions).\n\n- **Regression Testing**\n  - **Purpose:** Ensures new changes don’t break existing functionality.\n  - **When to use:** After bug fixes, updates, or new features are added.\n  - **Example:** Re-running all test cases after a code update to confirm no side effects.\n\n- **Sanity Testing**\n  - **Purpose:** Quick check to verify basic functionality after minor changes.\n  - **When to use:** After small fixes or builds (subset of regression testing).\n  - **Example:** Testing if a login button works after a UI tweak.\n\n- **Smoke Testing**\n  - **Purpose:** Basic test to ensure the system is stable enough for further testing.\n  -",
